
  The flow is a single loop: a write goes through [38;5;153mTaskStore[39m → the store emits a change → two subscribers react. The UI
  re-renders. The scheduler re-evaluates every task against [38;5;153mnow[39m and fires anything newly due-soon or overdue. Evaluation
  also runs on app load and on a timer while the page is open.

  [1mDeduplication.[0m Re-evaluating on every write means a task that's overdue would fire on every keystroke-triggered save.
  So dispatch is gated by a small record of what's already been sent — [38;5;153m(taskId, kind)[39m → timestamp, stored alongside the
  tasks. A given task fires "due soon" once and "overdue" once. Editing a task's due date clears its records, so moving
  a deadline legitimately re-arms both. This record is the only real state the notification system owns.

  [1mThe limitation, which I'd rather flag now than have you discover after it's built.[0m A service worker without a push
  server cannot reliably wake itself on a schedule. [38;5;153msetTimeout[39m doesn't survive worker shutdown, and Periodic Background
  Sync is Chrome/Edge-only, needs the app installed as a PWA, and lets the browser decide the interval — often many
  hours.

  What actually works reliably: notifications fire while the page is open, and on app open (catching everything missed
  since last visit). Background delivery is genuinely best-effort.

  So a task due at 9am notifies you at 9am [3mif the tab is open[0m, and otherwise the next time you open the app. That's
  meaningfully weaker than what "browser notifications" implies, and it's inherent to having no server — not something
  better implementation fixes.
